WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.860
Hi, I'm Ryan Sumner and I'm with the IBM
Cloud team.

00:00:02.860 --> 00:00:04.900
So, you're interested in VPC?

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:08.280
Well today I'm going to tell you how IBM
does VPC.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.140
Virtual Private Cloud, or VPC,

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:13.880
allows the user of the cloud environment

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.920
to define Virtual Private Networks,

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:19.180
manipulate them, control them,
tear them down, recreate them,

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:23.900
and then deploy workloads into those definitions.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.220
So, why does that really matter to you?

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.120
Why does it matter to the rest of the business?

00:00:28.120 --> 00:00:32.580
Well, agility, security, isolation,

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.780
performance, scalability - I could go on and on.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:38.790
But first I want to take you
through the building blocks that have

00:00:38.790 --> 00:00:43.340
broken out into a few key areas to help
you understand truly what it is.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:47.860
The first building block is all about the
core components that make up VPC.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:51.780
VPC is an isolated logical network that you create,

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.980
but the first thing that defines it is where it's located.

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:59.820
In IBM Cloud we call this location a Multizone Region,

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:01.300
or an MZR.

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:05.430
A Multizone Region consists
of at least three zones that are

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:09.930
completely fault tolerant locations
within that region that have absolutely

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:15.180
no dependence on the other zone and
allow you to deploy your applications in

00:01:15.180 --> 00:01:22.409
a resilient fashion. A VPC also defines
at a high level the IP addresses that

00:01:22.409 --> 00:01:28.409
will be used for private communication
within that VPC, again across that

00:01:28.409 --> 00:01:34.079
region. These IP addresses are further
used and broken down by allowing you to

00:01:34.079 --> 00:01:39.810
create one or more subnets within each
zone. So, now you're starting to build an

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:44.970
IP network. You have complete control
over this, create one subnet for a flat

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:51.210
network, or introduce segmentation with
multiple subnets again in each zone. The

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:55.020
next building block provides a component
to allow you to secure your cloud

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:59.040
deployments. Security Groups are a
network function that allows you to

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:05.670
create rules that will either allow or
deny network communication in, out, and

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:11.819
around the VPC. Now, that we've created
our VPC and we've secured it, we need to

00:02:11.819 --> 00:02:15.400
get connectivity to it.
IBM Cloud provides both public and

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:19.830
private access and different components
to provide connectivity for each of them.

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:24.730
For internet or public access, we provide
public gateway, that's your outbound

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:29.710
access. We also provide a floating IP
that you can tie to any of the cloud

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:34.020
resources that you've deployed in your VPC, providing both inbound and outbound

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:41.070
public access. For private connectivity,
we provide VPN as a Service, or

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:46.120
traditional direct link private circuits.
This is so you can extend your

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:51.280
enterprise into the IBM Cloud, or you can
even connect your VPC to another VPC,

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:56.110
or an outside resource. We're getting
close to deploying our workload in VPC

00:02:56.110 --> 00:03:00.130
but first let's talk about what network
functions exist to help us with

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:05.080
acceleration and availability. IBM Cloud
provides a Load Balancing as a Service,

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:11.500
which is an application load balancer
capable of both HTTP, HTTPS with SSL offload

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:16.570
and traditional TCP load balancing.
Now, that we've built our VPC we've

00:03:16.570 --> 00:03:20.740
secured it and we have connectivity in
and out of it, we can hand it over to our

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:24.910
application teams to deploy their
workloads. IBM Cloud VPC is enabled

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:28.120
today for virtual machines, deploy
customizable infrastructure, or

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:32.170
Kubernetes for those containerized
workloads. There are other capabilities

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:36.640
in VPC that provides security like
encrypt a block storage, or flexibility

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:41.800
like image import, and much more. I hope
all these building blocks help you

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:48.400
understand the value and benefits that
IBM Cloud VPC brings to you in your

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.560
public cloud journey. Thank you. If you
have any questions please drop us a line

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:57.370
below. If you want to see more videos
like this in the future please like and

00:03:57.370 --> 00:04:01.390
subscribe and don't forget you can
always get started on the cloud at no

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:05.670
cost by signing up for a free IBM Cloud
account.

